[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was marked known: one bug, one fix queued. Its reports describe three
problems. The subjects look alike, so the desk merged them on subject
([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)). Each problem has its own ticket.

The desk closed #4471 on 5 October. Its update ends two of the three: `parser` is fixed
(`fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`), and `hint` won't be fixed, because the booking-v3
form is retired. The update says nothing about `timezone`, so that ticket stays open.

<CauseTickets ticket={4471} filed={tickets} />

<Callout kind="warn">
The parser fix does not touch the one-day moves. Their bodies show no swap. If they keep coming
in, they belong on the open ticket, not on #4471.
</Callout>

## How to tell them apart

Read the body, not the subject or the intake tag. Intake guesses from the reporter's browser; the
cause depends on the tenant, its booking form and the channel (`causes.ts`).

- **`parser`**: day and month swapped ("7 April went out on 4 July"). The shared parser reads
  every date month first, and every tenant on file writes the day first. Comes in by app, email,
  or a `booking-v2` form.
- **`hint`**: the same swap, but only through the booking form of a `booking-v3` tenant. That
  form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field, people type what it says, and the form then
  reads the date day first. Fixing the parser did not change the hint; retiring the form did.
- **`timezone`**: nothing swapped. The date lands one day early or late ("Tuesday 12 May,
  calendar says Monday 11 May"), an invite arrives before the booking, or one meeting falls on
  different days in Lisbon and Toronto. The desk read these as one-day moves
  (`desk-notes.json`) and filed them under `parser`; here they are counted on their own.

A few bodies name no dates ("sent my mother to the dentist on the wrong day"). They are counted
by tenant and channel, like the rest.

